{"verdict": "add", "record": {"a": "02-electromagnetism-and-optics/classical-optics/polarization-and-crystal-optics", "b": "02-electromagnetism-and-optics/photonics-and-lasers/nonlinear-optics", "name": "Birefringent phase matching", "kind": "mechanism", "summary": "Normal dispersion makes n(2ω)>n(ω), so in an isotropic medium Δk≠0. In a uniaxial crystal, choosing the harmonic on the extraordinary axis at an angle where n_e(2ω,θ)=n_o(ω) gives Δk=0. Spatial walk-off of the Poynting vector limits the useful crystal length.", "map": "n_e(2\\omega,\\theta_m)=n_o(\\omega),\\quad \\frac{1}{n_e^2(\\theta)}=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{n_o^2}+\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{n_e^2}", "breaks": "", "confidence": "established", "strength": 3, "sources": []}}
{"verdict": "add", "record": {"a": "02-electromagnetism-and-optics/photonics-and-lasers/nonlinear-optics", "b": "02-electromagnetism-and-optics/photonics-and-lasers/laser-physics", "name": "Kerr-lens mode-locking", "kind": "mechanism", "summary": "In a Ti:sapphire laser, the intensity-dependent index n0+n2I focuses intense pulse peaks more strongly than CW light. An aperture or gain-overlap effect converts this into a fast saturable loss that favors pulsed operation. Intracavity SPM balanced against negative GDD then shapes few-cycle soliton-like pulses.", "map": "n=n_0+n_2I\\Rightarrow f_{\\rm Kerr}^{-1}\\propto n_2I/w^2", "breaks": "", "confidence": "established", "strength": 3, "sources": []}}
{"verdict": "add", "record": {"a": "02-electromagnetism-and-optics/electrodynamics/electromagnetic-waves-and-waveguides", "b": "02-electromagnetism-and-optics/photonics-and-lasers/metamaterials-and-plasmonics", "name": "Surface plasmon polariton as a pole of the TM Fresnel coefficient", "kind": "mechanism", "summary": "At a metal–dielectric interface with ε_m<−ε_d, the p-polarized Fresnel coefficient has a pole beyond the light line. That pole is a bound TM surface wave evanescent on both sides, the SPP. It is excited by prism (Kretschmann/Otto) or grating coupling to supply the missing momentum.", "map": "r_p\\to\\infty\\iff\\frac{k_{z,d}}{\\epsilon_d}+\\frac{k_{z,m}}{\\epsilon_m}=0\\Rightarrow k_{\\rm SPP}=\\frac{\\omega}{c}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\epsilon_m\\epsilon_d}{\\epsilon_m+\\epsilon_d}}", "breaks": "", "confidence": "established", "strength": 3, "sources": []}}
{"verdict": "add", "record": {"a": "02-electromagnetism-and-optics/radiation-and-scattering/radiation-from-moving-charges", "b": "02-electromagnetism-and-optics/radiation-and-scattering/cherenkov-and-transition-radiation", "name": "Transition radiation as a charge meeting its image", "kind": "mechanism", "summary": "A uniformly moving charge crossing into a conductor radiates as its field reorganizes, like a charge and its image annihilating abruptly. Ginzburg–Frank theory gives emission peaked at angle ~1/γ and a total yield proportional to γ, which is the basis of TRDs.", "map": "\\theta_{\\rm peak}\\sim1/\\gamma,\\quad W\\propto\\alpha\\,\\gamma\\,\\hbar\\omega_p", "breaks": "", "confidence": "established", "strength": 2, "sources": []}}
{"verdict": "add", "record": {"a": "00-foundations/measurement-and-data-analysis/detectors-and-instrumentation", "b": "02-electromagnetism-and-optics/radiation-and-scattering/cherenkov-and-transition-radiation", "name": "RICH particle identification from the Cherenkov angle", "kind": "method transfer", "summary": "A ring-imaging Cherenkov detector focuses the emission cone into a ring whose radius gives θ_C and hence β. Combined with momentum from tracking, this yields the particle's mass, separating π, K and p. Photon yield follows Frank–Tamm.", "map": "\\cos\\theta_C=\\frac{1}{n\\beta},\\quad m=\\frac{p}{c}\\sqrt{n^2\\cos^2\\theta_C-1}", "breaks": "", "confidence": "established", "strength": 2, "sources": []}}
{"verdict": "add", "record": {"a": "00-foundations/symmetry-and-group-theory/discrete-symmetries-and-cpt", "b": "02-electromagnetism-and-optics/radiation-and-scattering/dipole-radiation-and-antennas", "name": "Lorentz reciprocity and how to break it", "kind": "mechanism", "summary": "Linear, time-invariant media with symmetric ε and μ tensors obey Lorentz reciprocity, so antennas transmit and receive with the same pattern. Isolation and one-way devices need broken reciprocity: magneto-optic bias (T-odd), time modulation, or nonlinearity.", "map": "\\int(\\mathbf J_1\\cdot\\mathbf E_2-\\mathbf J_2\\cdot\\mathbf E_1)\\,dV=0\\iff\\epsilon=\\epsilon^T,\\ \\mu=\\mu^T", "breaks": "", "confidence": "established", "strength": 2, "sources": []}}
